      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. 12,750 mg/day of metformin is about 5 times the maximum labeled dose. It has no therapeutic benefit beyond the usual ceiling. It carries a serious risk of life-threatening lactic acidosis, even in a patient with normal kidney and liver function. I can't treat it as a routine order.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter a dose within the labeled range.** For example, 850 mg PO three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day), or a lower starting dose with titration.\n- **Help you with an inpatient glycemic plan.** If the goal is tighter glucose control, insulin or another agent is usually more appropriate than going above the metformin maximum.\n- **Route the order through pharmacy or your attending for review.** Use this option if you think there's a special circumstance I don't know about. The order would need to be placed through a process with that oversight.\n\nTell me which dose you'd like and I'll enter it."
      }
